From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Builder <builder@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vout: accept vout= and connector0_type= as module parameters

The command line handlers for vout= and connector0_type= are registered
with __setup(), which only takes effect for built-in code. This driver is
compiled into the aml_media module (CONFIG_AMLOGIC_MEDIA_MODULE=m,
CONFIG_AMLOGIC_VOUT_SERVE=y), so nothing claims either parameter -- the
kernel reports them as "Unknown kernel command line parameters" and passes
them to user space.

The consequence is that get_vout_mode_uboot() keeps its "null" default
even when the bootloader has already enabled the display. The logo path
in the DRM driver overwrites its own outputmode with that value and then
bails out with "NULL MODE or DUMMY MODE, nothing to do", so no mode is
ever programmed: fbcon draws into a plane that is never routed to a
connector, and the display stays blank until some DRM master performs a
modeset of its own.

REBASE NOTE (6.12) -- this patch was restructured, not re-offsetted.

The previous version of this patch did three things by hand: it moved the
parsing helpers out of an #ifndef MODULE block so they would be compiled
in a modular build, it kept __setup() behind #ifndef MODULE, and it added
module_param_named() charp parameters plus a vout_parse_module_params()
called from vout_init_module(). None of that is the right shape against
6.12, because upstream has already done the first two and has written the
third:

  - The #ifndef MODULE block is gone. vout_serve.c now stubs the macro
    instead, at the top of the file:
        #ifdef MODULE
        #undef __setup
        #define __setup(_str, _fn)
        #endif
    so str2lower(), vout_init_mode_parse(), get_vout_init_mode(),
    get_connector0_type() and get_connector_type_to_compat() are already
    compiled unconditionally and the __setup() lines already collapse to
    nothing in a modular build. There is nothing left for the old hunks 2
    to 5 to do, and re-applying them would be redundant.

  - Upstream added exactly the parameter plumbing this patch used to add,
    as ODROID_VOUT_DEFINE_STRING_PARAM/ODROID_VOUT_REGISTER_PARAM in
    vout_func.h and a block of module_param_cb() registrations at the end
    of vout_serve.c. It registers vout, connector0_type, connector_type
    and vout_print.

  - But that work is behind #ifdef CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON, which
    is a promptless bool selected only by ARCH_MESON_ODROIDC5. This board
    config does not select it, so the entire block is compiled out and the
    original defect is still present.

So the rebase reduces to: stop gating the upstream implementation on a
config symbol this board does not set. Two guards are removed -- the one
around the macro definitions in vout_func.h and the one around the
registrations in vout_serve.c -- and nothing else changes. The resulting
code is the code upstream already ships for its own boards, so the parsing
and storage semantics are upstream's rather than a reimplementation of
them. Removing the guard cannot regress a configuration that did set
CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON, because for those the block was already
being compiled.

Judgement call: the alternative was to leave upstream's block alone and
add a second, self-contained implementation under
#ifndef CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON. That was rejected -- it would
duplicate about sixty lines whose only merit is being identical to the
lines above them, and two registrations of the same parameter names in one
module would then have to be kept mutually exclusive by hand forever.
The cost of the chosen form is that vout_func.h is touched as well as
vout_serve.c, and that the identifiers and MODULE_PARM_DESC strings keep
their upstream names on a board that is not an ODROID. That is cosmetic
and was preferred over the duplication.

get_vout_init_mode() and the string it is handed:

Its 6.12 parsing still mutates its argument in place. It scans for the
first character that is not alphanumeric, '_' or '-', takes that character
as the separator, and then walks the string with strsep(), which writes
NULs into it; each field is then lowercased in place by str2lower(). It is
now non-static and returns -EINVAL rather than 0 when the string holds no
separator at all, but it is no less destructive than before. A charp
module parameter points at memory that must not be scribbled on, which is
why the previous version of this patch kstrdup()ed before calling it.

That kstrdup() is no longer required, and is not carried over. Upstream's
setter, odroid_vout_set_parser_string(), copies the incoming value into a
VMODE_NAME_LEN_MAX stack buffer with strscpy() and passes the copy to the
parser, so the caller's string is never touched. A stack copy is a valid
substitute for the heap copy here because VMODE_NAME_LEN_MAX (64) is the
same bound vout_mode_uboot and connector0_type are already declared with,
so a value too long to survive strscpy() truncation was never storable
anyway, and because the copy is not retained past the call -- the parser
extracts what it needs into vout_mode_uboot/uboot_display before
returning. It also cannot fail, so the "kstrdup returned NULL, silently do
nothing" path the old patch had is gone.

Timing also changes and is worth stating: a module_param_cb() .set
callback runs while module parameters are being parsed, which is before
the module init function runs. The old patch instead called
vout_parse_module_params() from vout_init_module(). Both are early enough
-- what matters is only that the mode is resolved before
platform_driver_register() lets vout probe -- so the new form is
equivalent and slightly earlier. vout_init_module() does still exist under
that name; it is simply no longer a useful hook, so it is left alone.

One sharp edge that comes with reusing upstream's setter, recorded here
because it is a severe failure mode and it is not obvious from the diff:
odroid_vout_set_parser_string() propagates a negative parser return, and
get_vout_init_mode() returns -EINVAL when its argument contains no
separator -- that is, no character outside [A-Za-z0-9_-]. A module
parameter .set callback that returns an error aborts the module load, so
aml_media.vout=1080p60hz on its own would stop aml_media loading at all,
which costs the display and the rest of the media stack rather than just
the mode. A value such as 1080p60hz,disable parses fine because the comma
is the separator.

This is upstream's behaviour rather than something introduced here, and
boot/boot.ini is safe against it: the bootargs line spells the parameter
as aml_media.vout=${outputmode},disable, so the comma is present whatever
${outputmode} is set to. It is only reachable by editing that bootargs
line to drop the suffix. Note this differs from the previous version of
this patch, which called get_vout_init_mode() from vout_init_module() and
discarded its return, so a separator-less value was silently ignored
instead of being fatal. The stricter behaviour was accepted as the price
of reusing upstream's implementation rather than maintaining a parallel
one.

Why all four parameters and not just vout=:

get_connector0_type(), get_connector_type_to_compat() and
vout_print_enable() are all static, and with __setup() stubbed their only
reference is gone. Registering them as parameters is what gives them a
caller again; without it a -Werror build (the tree adds -Werror
unconditionally for CONFIG_AMLOGIC_DRIVER) fails on unused-function.
connector_type= and vout_print= are therefore carried for that reason
rather than because the board needs them. get_vout_init_mode() would not
have needed this -- it is non-static in 6.12 -- but it is the one that
matters for the display.

With the guard removed the parameters are reachable as aml_media.vout=,
aml_media.connector0_type=, aml_media.connector_type= and
aml_media.vout_print= (KBUILD_MODNAME for this file is aml_media). These
are module parameters, so the bare vout= spelling that works for a
built-in kernel does not reach them -- the boot arguments have to name the
module. boot/boot.ini already passes aml_media.vout= and
aml_media.connector0_type=, and the names registered here match it, so no
boot side change is needed. Delivery of a module parameter from the kernel
command line to a loadable module is done by kmod, which applies matching
modname.option= entries from /proc/cmdline when the module is loaded; that
path is assumed to work here and has not been exercised on hardware.

vout2_serve.c and vout3_serve.c carry the same defect and are un-gated the
same way. Each has its own stubbed __setup() pair and its own copy of the
registration block behind CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON, so with the
guard left in place get_vout2_init_mode(), get_connector1_type(),
get_vout3_init_mode() and get_connector2_type() are static with no caller
and -Werror stops the build on unused-function, exactly as it does for
vout_serve.c. This board drives one display pipe, so the vout2= and vout3=
parameters are not expected to be used; they are registered because that is
what keeps those files compiling, and because leaving two of the three
gated would be an arbitrary split. An unused module parameter costs a name
in sysfs and nothing else.
---
--- a/common_drivers/drivers/media/vout/vout_serve/vout_func.h
+++ b/common_drivers/drivers/media/vout/vout_serve/vout_func.h
@@ -93,7 +93,11 @@
 int vout3_shutdown(void);
 #endif
 
-#ifdef CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON
+/*
+ * These only define macros, so they cost nothing where they are unused.
+ * They are needed by vout_serve.c on every configuration, not just the
+ * ones that set CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON, so do not gate them.
+ */
 
 #define ODROID_VOUT_DEFINE_STRING_PARAM(_prefix, _getter, _name, _parser, _store, _size) \
 static int odroid_##_prefix##_set_##_name(const char *val,				\
@@ -124,5 +128,3 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(_name, _desc)
 
 #endif
-
-#endif
--- a/common_drivers/drivers/media/vout/vout_serve/vout_serve.c
+++ b/common_drivers/drivers/media/vout/vout_serve/vout_serve.c
@@ -1421,8 +1421,15 @@
 
 __setup("connector_type=", get_connector_type_to_compat);
 
-#ifdef CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON
-/* module parameter support */
+/*
+ * Module parameter support. Not gated on CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON:
+ * this file is linked into aml_media, which is a module here, so the
+ * __setup() handlers above expand to nothing (see the #undef at the top of
+ * this file, and init.h for the generic case). Without the parameters below
+ * nothing claims vout= or connector0_type=, get_vout_mode_uboot() keeps its
+ * "null" default, and the three handlers are static functions with no
+ * caller left, which -Werror rejects.
+ */
 static char odroid_vout_param[VMODE_NAME_LEN_MAX];
 
 static int odroid_vout_get_string(char *buf, const struct kernel_param *kp)
@@ -1480,7 +1487,6 @@
 			   "ODROID compatibility connector type for aml_media");
 ODROID_VOUT_REGISTER_PARAM(vout, vout_print, &vout_debug_print,
 			   "ODROID vout debug print enable");
-#endif
 
 /*TODO: drm to disable display/mode sysfs set.*/
 void disable_vout_mode_set_sysfs(void)
--- a/common_drivers/drivers/media/vout/vout_serve/vout2_serve.c
+++ b/common_drivers/drivers/media/vout/vout_serve/vout2_serve.c
@@ -1102,8 +1102,13 @@
 
 __setup("connector1_type=", get_connector1_type);
 
-#ifdef CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON
-/* module parameter support */
+/*
+ * Module parameter support, un-gated for the same reason as vout_serve.c:
+ * this file is linked into aml_media, which is a module here, so the
+ * __setup() handler above expands to nothing, leaving get_vout2_init_mode()
+ * and get_connector1_type() as static functions with no caller, which
+ * -Werror rejects.
+ */
 static char odroid_vout2_param[VMODE_NAME_LEN_MAX];
 
 static int odroid_vout2_get_string(char *buf, const struct kernel_param *kp)
@@ -1145,7 +1150,6 @@
 			   "ODROID secondary display mode for aml_media");
 ODROID_VOUT_REGISTER_PARAM(vout2, connector1_type, connector1_type,
 			   "ODROID secondary connector type for aml_media");
-#endif
 
 //MODULE_AUTHOR("Platform-BJ <platform.bj@amlogic.com>");
 //MODULE_DESCRIPTION("VOUT2 Server Module");
--- a/common_drivers/drivers/media/vout/vout_serve/vout3_serve.c
+++ b/common_drivers/drivers/media/vout/vout_serve/vout3_serve.c
@@ -1103,8 +1103,13 @@
 
 __setup("connector2_type=", get_connector2_type);
 
-#ifdef CONFIG_ARCH_MESON_ODROID_COMMON
-/* module parameter support */
+/*
+ * Module parameter support, un-gated for the same reason as vout_serve.c:
+ * this file is linked into aml_media, which is a module here, so the
+ * __setup() handler above expands to nothing, leaving get_vout3_init_mode()
+ * and get_connector2_type() as static functions with no caller, which
+ * -Werror rejects.
+ */
 static char odroid_vout3_param[VMODE_NAME_LEN_MAX];
 
 static int odroid_vout3_get_string(char *buf, const struct kernel_param *kp)
@@ -1146,7 +1151,6 @@
 			   "ODROID tertiary display mode for aml_media");
 ODROID_VOUT_REGISTER_PARAM(vout3, connector2_type, connector2_type,
 			   "ODROID tertiary connector type for aml_media");
-#endif
 
 //MODULE_AUTHOR("Platform-BJ <platform.bj@amlogic.com>");
 //MODULE_DESCRIPTION("vout3 Server Module");
